1. Create a text file and insert data in Linux
cat > users.txt <<'EOF'
2025-11-03,User1,Age=21;state=CA;gender=M;
2025-11-03,User2,Age=25;state=TX;gender=F;
2025-11-03,User3,Age=32;state=CA;gender=M;
2025-11-03,User4,Age=18;state=NY;gender=F;
2025-11-03,User5,Age=40;state=TX;gender=M;
EOF


2. Display the file
cat users.txt


3. Create an input directory in HDFS
hdfs dfs -mkdir -p /home/cloudera/input


4. Upload the file to HDFS
hdfs dfs -put -f /home/cloudera/users.txt /home/cloudera/input/


5. Verify the file in HDFS
hdfs dfs -ls /home/cloudera/input


hdfs dfs -cat /home/cloudera/input/users.txt


6. Open Hive
hive


7. Create a database
CREATE DATABASE IF NOT EXISTS user_analysis;


8. Select the database
USE user_analysis;


9. Create an external Hive table
CREATE EXTERNAL TABLE IF NOT EXISTS userdata (
    data_date STRING,
    user_id STRING,
    properties STRING
)
ROW FORMAT DELIMITED
FIELDS TERMINATED BY ','
STORED AS TEXTFILE
LOCATION '/user/cloudera/hive_lab/input';

Note: The screenshot's Hive table location is /user/cloudera/hive_lab/input, which differs from the HDFS upload directory /home/cloudera/input. To make the table read the uploaded file, use the same location for both. You can instead upload the file to the table's location:
hdfs dfs -mkdir -p /user/cloudera/hive_lab/input
hdfs dfs -put -f /home/cloudera/users.txt /user/cloudera/hive_lab/input/


10. Display Hive tables
SHOW TABLES;


11. View the table data
SELECT * FROM userdata;


12. Extract age, state, and gender using regular expressions
CREATE TABLE userdata_parsed AS
SELECT
    data_date,
    user_id,
    regexp_extract(properties, 'Age=([0-9]+)', 1) AS age,
    regexp_extract(properties, 'state=([A-Z]+)', 1) AS state,
    regexp_extract(properties, 'gender=([A-Z]+)', 1) AS gender
FROM userdata;


13. Display the extracted results
SELECT * FROM userdata_parsed;





















